WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.373
Nine a.m.

2
00:00:03.373 --> 00:00:05.020
Your manager stops by.

3
00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.789
Traffic to the shop jumped overnight.

4
00:00:07.789 --> 00:00:10.707
Who is calling us, and what is failing?

5
00:00:10.707 --> 00:00:12.952
A short report by ten, please.

6
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:16.568
You have the web server's logbook, a few hundred lines.

7
00:00:16.568 --> 00:00:20.711
grep can pick out lines, but the manager wants counts and rankings.

8
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:26.801
The first idea is to copy everything into a spreadsheet and count by hand.

9
00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:27.514
It works.

10
00:00:27.514 --> 00:00:28.545
By lunchtime.

11
00:00:28.545 --> 00:00:29.259
Tomorrow.

12
00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:33.420
There's a better way, and it starts with how paper moves between desks.

13
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.162
Every desk in the building has three trays.

14
00:00:36.162 --> 00:00:38.161
Work comes in through the in-tray.

15
00:00:38.161 --> 00:00:40.160
Finished pages go in the out-tray.

16
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:44.299
And complaints go in a third tray, so they never mix with the good work.

17
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.472
Every Linux program has the same three trays, called streams.

18
00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:51.532
Standard input, number zero, is where it reads.

19
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:55.328
Standard output, number one, is where it writes its results.

20
00:00:55.328 --> 00:00:59.043
Standard error, number two, is where it writes its complaints.

21
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:03.721
By default, input is your keyboard, and both outputs go to your screen.

22
00:01:03.721 --> 00:01:08.112
And here's the key: the shell that starts a program sets up its trays.

23
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:11.940
The program writes to tray one the same way, wherever it leads.

24
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.176
So the shell can point a tray somewhere else, and the program never knows.

25
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.577
Pointing a tray at a file is called redirection.

26
00:01:19.577 --> 00:01:25.203
Here the shell opens note dot text, points tray one at it, and only then starts echo.

27
00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:27.778
One greater than replaces the file.

28
00:01:27.778 --> 00:01:29.051
Two add to the end.

29
00:01:29.051 --> 00:01:35.885
And two, greater than, dev null, sends complaints to the bin: a special file that discards everything.

30
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:37.559
Now a guess.

31
00:01:37.559 --> 00:01:42.928
To sort a file in place, you type: sort names.txt, greater than, names.txt.

32
00:01:42.928 --> 00:01:45.147
What is in the file afterwards?

33
00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:48.916
Nothing.

34
00:01:48.916 --> 00:01:49.672
It's empty.

35
00:01:49.672 --> 00:01:56.472
The shell sets up the trays before the program starts, and opening the file for writing empties it.

36
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.368
Only then does sort start.

37
00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:02.089
It reads the file, finds nothing, and writes nothing back.

38
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.000
The shell is very efficient.

39
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.156
It cleared the desk before the worker even sat down.

40
00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:11.697
So: a single greater than empties the file before the command starts.

41
00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:14.521
Never redirect into the file you are reading.

42
00:02:14.521 --> 00:02:17.157
Write a new file, then move it into place.

43
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:20.828
A better place to send a tray is another desk.

44
00:02:20.828 --> 00:02:24.871
Imagine a chute from one out-tray straight into the next in-tray.

45
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.261
That chute is the pipe, the vertical bar.

46
00:02:28.261 --> 00:02:34.121
grep picks the error lines, the pipe drops them into wc, and wc counts them.

47
00:02:34.121 --> 00:02:34.507
Four.

48
00:02:34.733 --> 00:02:37.278
Notice, only tray one goes down the chute.

49
00:02:37.278 --> 00:02:42.064
Complaints skip it and land on your screen, which is exactly what they are for.

50
00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:44.703
Neither worker knows the other exists.

51
00:02:44.703 --> 00:02:49.960
Programs that read lines, keep or change some, and write lines are called filters.

52
00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:56.576
Now the manager's report, built one desk at a time, looking at the output after each step.

53
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.352
Desk one is awk, a tool for columns.

54
00:02:59.352 --> 00:03:08.781
Dollar one is the first column, and in access.log that's the client's IP address, the number that identifies a computer on a network.

55
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.455
One IP per request.

56
00:03:11.455 --> 00:03:12.747
238 lines.

57
00:03:12.967 --> 00:03:14.607
We want a count per IP.

58
00:03:14.607 --> 00:03:17.532
uniq, with dash C, counts repeated lines.

59
00:03:17.532 --> 00:03:19.387
Let's drop it straight in.

60
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.462
And the counts are ones, with the odd two or three.

61
00:03:23.462 --> 00:03:27.172
198 lines of counts, for only nine different IPs.

62
00:03:27.172 --> 00:03:27.550
Well.

63
00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.973
That's not the report we wanted.

64
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:30.931
Here's why.

65
00:03:30.931 --> 00:03:35.652
uniq merges repeated lines only when they sit right next to each other.

66
00:03:35.652 --> 00:03:39.308
It remembers just the line before, which keeps it fast.

67
00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:44.105
It is the colleague who only remembers the last person who walked past.

68
00:03:44.105 --> 00:03:48.933
The same visitor came back sixty-one times, but hardly ever twice in a row.

69
00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:53.305
So the point is: put the same IPs next to each other first.

70
00:03:53.305 --> 00:03:54.919
That is a job for sort.

71
00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:56.897
Insert sort before uniq.

72
00:03:56.897 --> 00:04:01.013
Now identical IPs sit together, and uniq can count them.

73
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:04.305
Nine IPs, each with a real count.

74
00:04:04.305 --> 00:04:09.332
Notice that sort orders text, so 10.0.0.2 comes first.

75
00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:10.550
Now rank them.

76
00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:14.688
sort dash R N sorts the counts as numbers, largest first.

77
00:04:14.688 --> 00:04:16.721
And head keeps the top five.

78
00:04:16.933 --> 00:04:19.374
There is your first answer.

79
00:04:19.374 --> 00:04:22.899
One client sent 61 of the 238 requests.

80
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:28.625
Save it to report dot text with one greater than, then add the failing pages with two.

81
00:04:28.625 --> 00:04:32.159
Use one twice by mistake, and the first answer is gone.

82
00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:35.381
Notice how we got here: one desk at a time.

83
00:04:35.381 --> 00:04:40.609
That's how we caught uniq in the act, instead of hunting for a broken step at the end.

84
00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:44.680
And the honest limit: these tools read one machine's files.

85
00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.488
For hundreds of servers, you need a central place for logs, later in the course.

86
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.050
So, the moral of the story.

87
00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:57.428
Every program has three trays, and the shell points them before the program starts.

88
00:04:57.428 --> 00:05:01.057
A pipe is a chute from one out-tray to the next in-tray.

89
00:05:01.057 --> 00:05:04.556
Small filters, chained, turn raw lines into an answer.

90
00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:06.213
A quick check.

91
00:05:06.213 --> 00:05:11.619
A job sends its output to backup dot log, but its errors still appear on screen.

92
00:05:11.619 --> 00:05:17.702
Read two, greater than, ampersand one, as: send tray two wherever tray one goes right now.

93
00:05:17.702 --> 00:05:19.053
Which line fixes it?

94
00:05:22.267 --> 00:05:23.237
The second one.

95
00:05:23.237 --> 00:05:26.018
The shell reads redirections left to right.

96
00:05:26.018 --> 00:05:30.287
First tray one points at the file, then tray two follows it there.
